The race occurs across `await cancel()`: an older search can resume after a newer search and overwrite its subscription. Invalidate older work **before awaiting**, detach the previous subscription, and check the generation before attaching or publishing.

```dart
StreamSubscription<SearchResult>? _searchSubscription;
int _searchGeneration = 0;
bool _closed = false;

Future<void> search(String query) async {
  if (_closed) return;

  final generation = ++_searchGeneration;
  final previous = _searchSubscription;
  _searchSubscription = null;

  // Cancellation failures propagate to the caller.
  await previous?.cancel();

  bool isCurrent() =>
      !_closed && generation == _searchGeneration;

  if (!isCurrent()) return;

  _searchSubscription = repository.search(query).listen(
    (result) {
      if (isCurrent()) emitResult(result);
    },
    onError: (Object error, StackTrace stackTrace) {
      if (isCurrent()) emitError(error, stackTrace);
    },
  );
}

Future<void> close() async {
  if (_closed) return;

  _closed = true;
  ++_searchGeneration;

  final subscription = _searchSubscription;
  _searchSubscription = null;
  await subscription?.cancel();
}
```

Keep these fields and methods on the object that owns the search lifecycle. Call `close()` at teardown; callers must await or explicitly handle failures from `search()` and `close()`. If this owner creates an output controller, it must also close it.

Verify with controllable cancellation futures:

- Pause query A during cancellation, start B, then release A: only B attaches.
- Deliver obsolete results and errors: neither reaches consumers.
- Close while cancellation is pending: no listener attaches afterward.
- Check normal results, stream errors with stack traces, and cancellation failures.

No RxDart or debounce is needed to fix this ordering bug. I haven’t run tests or static analysis here.
